---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Diagram from "../components/Diagram.astro";
import Shot from "../components/Shot.astro";

const nodes = [
  ["understand", "Regular expressions extract order, customer and product IDs and emails; a small model adds a one-line summary and a request type. The regex wins on IDs.", "small model"],
  ["load_memory", "Loads up to ten long-term memories for the customers involved, including owners of referenced orders, plus the operator's. Preferences come before interaction summaries.", "none"],
  ["retrieve", "Two parallel waves across the four sources return evidence items with stable IDs, conflict notes and missing-data notes.", "none"],
  ["reason_and_plan", "Builds the planning prompt and parses a structured decision: answer, act, clarify, escalate or refuse, with cited analysis and typed steps. Off-domain requests are refused here without a model call.", "large model"],
  ["validate", "Checks every citation against the retrieved evidence, validates tool arguments and step dependencies, classifies risk, writes proposed entries to the audit log and runs the business preconditions.", "none"],
  ["approval_gate", "Collects the steps that the risk policy says need a person and logs each approval request.", "none"],
  ["await_approval", "Pauses the graph with a LangGraph interrupt. The run is checkpointed in SQLite and resumes when the operator approves or rejects each step.", "none"],
  ["execute", "Runs the steps layer by layer, independent steps in parallel on a thread pool, re-checking preconditions just before each tool call.", "none"],
  ["observe", "Adds read-tool results to the evidence and decides whether to plan again, at most three iterations in total.", "none"],
  ["respond", "Writes the operator-facing explanation. Evidence IDs are rewritten as readable references in code.", "large model"],
  ["save_memory", "Extracts durable facts such as a refund-method preference and stores a one-line summary per customer.", "small model"],
];
---

<Base path="/how-it-works">
  <header class="page-head">
    <p class="kicker">How it works</p>
    <h1>Eleven steps from a typed request to an explained action</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      OpsPilot is a LangGraph state machine. Each request passes through the same eleven steps, and each step
      writes what it did to a trace that the operator can open.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="graph">
    <p class="section-label">The agent graph</p>
    <h2 id="graph">One request, end to end</h2>
    <div class="row">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The graph starts by working out which orders, customers and products a request is about, then gathers
          everything the store knows about them. The large model plans only after retrieval, and code validates
          the plan before anything can run. High-risk steps stop at an interrupt until a person decides.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="note" aria-label="Side note: Why LangGraph">
        <span class="note-label" aria-hidden="true">Why LangGraph</span>
        Its interrupt and resume primitives, with a SQLite checkpointer, let an approval wait indefinitely and
        survive a restart, and its conditional edges express the replanning loop directly.
      </aside>
    </div>
    <Diagram
      id="agent-graph"
      number={1}
      caption="The agent graph from docs/architecture.md, rendered to SVG at build time. Dotted lines mark steps that write to the audit log."
    />
    <section class="table-scroll" aria-label="The eleven graph steps" tabindex="0">
      <table>
        <caption>What each step does, and which model tier it calls.</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Step</th><th scope="col">What it does</th><th scope="col">Model</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {nodes.map(([name, what, model]) => (
            <tr><td class="mono">{name}</td><td>{what}</td><td>{model}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="knowledge">
    <p class="section-label">Knowledge</p>
    <h2 id="knowledge">Four sources, one evidence list</h2>
    <div class="row">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Policies live in Chroma as one chunk per numbered section, embedded locally with the ONNX
          all-MiniLM-L6-v2 model, so no embedding API is involved. Orders, customers, refunds and tickets live in
          SQLite. A warehouse CSV feed carries stock counts with snapshot times, and a JSON FAQ is indexed next to
          the policies.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every retrieved fact becomes an evidence item with a stable ID, such as
          <code>policy:return_policy_v2§2</code>, <code>db:orders:ORD-1001</code>,
          <code>csv:inventory:EL-HP-02</code> or <code>memory:12</code>. The planner may cite only these IDs, and
          the validation step rejects any other.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="note" aria-label="Side note: Computed facts">
        <span class="note-label" aria-hidden="true">Computed facts</span>
        Order evidence includes days since delivery, days past the delivery promise and the amount still
        refundable, so the model does not do date or money arithmetic on its own.
      </aside>
    </div>
    <Diagram
      id="knowledge"
      number={2}
      caption="Where the knowledge comes from and where code sits between the plan and the database."
    />
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="conflicts">
    <p class="section-label">Incomplete and conflicting information</p>
    <h2 id="conflicts">Old policies stay visible, but only the current one applies</h2>
    <div class="row">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Return Policy version 1 allowed 30 days for everything. Version 2, effective 1 March 2026, allows 14 days
          for electronics and makes grocery non-returnable. When a version 1 section is retrieved, the retriever
          also fetches its current counterpart from version 2, marks the old chunk as superseded and records a
          conflict note. The model sees both; validation refuses any step that relies on the superseded one.
        </p>
        <p>
          The same idea covers data. When the database says a product has 5 units but the fresher warehouse feed
          says 0, both values stay in the evidence and the fresher one wins. When an order has no delivery date,
          the gap is listed as missing information, and the agent escalates instead of guessing.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="note" aria-label="Side note: See also">
        <span class="note-label" aria-hidden="true">See also</span>
        The <a href="/scenarios">scenarios page</a> shows the stock conflict (scenario 7) and the missing delivery
        date (scenario 6) as the agent handled them.
      </aside>
    </div>
    <div class="shots-2">
      <Shot
        file="sources-superseded.png"
        number={3}
        alt="Sources panel for the return-window question: Return Policy v2 section 2 is listed normally, while the retrieved Return Policy v1 sections are struck through and labelled superseded, not applied."
        caption="Sources for 'What is the return window for Nova Buds Pro headphones?'. Version 1 sections are shown but struck through."
      />
      <Shot
        file="conflict.png"
        number={4}
        alt="Reasoning panel for an exchange request: an inventory conflict note says the database reports 5 units and the warehouse feed reports 0, and resolves to the fresher feed; a missing-information note says ORD-1004 has no delivery date."
        caption="An exchange request with conflicting stock counts and a missing delivery date. Both are surfaced before the agent decides."
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="execution">
    <p class="section-label">Execution</p>
    <h2 id="execution">Parallel where possible, ordered where needed</h2>
    <div class="row">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Plan steps declare dependencies with <code>depends_on</code>. The executor sorts them into layers and
          runs each layer on a pool of four threads, so a coupon and a ticket can run together while the email that
          reports them waits. A step whose dependency failed or was rejected is skipped with the reason recorded.
          Each tool call opens its own SQLite connection in WAL mode.
        </p>
        <p>
          If a step is blocked or fails, the observe step sends the results back to the planner, which sees the
          reason (for example "outside the 30-day apparel return window") and can choose an alternative such as a
          goodwill coupon. A step identical to one that already ran is skipped, and refunds carry an idempotency key,
          so replanning cannot act twice.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="approval">
    <p class="section-label">Approvals</p>
    <h2 id="approval">A paused run waits in a checkpoint</h2>
    <div class="row">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          When a step needs a person, the graph stops at an interrupt and its full state is saved to SQLite under the
          session ID. The operator can approve or reject each step, with a note, from the app or the command line;
          the run then resumes where it stopped and records every decision in the audit log.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Diagram
      id="approval-sequence"
      number={5}
      caption="The approval sequence: the graph checkpoints at the interrupt, and the operator's decision resumes it."
    />
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="memory">
    <p class="section-label">Memory</p>
    <h2 id="memory">Durable facts carry across sessions</h2>
    <div class="row">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          After each turn a small model extracts durable facts, for example that a customer prefers store credit.
          Memories are stored in SQLite with a uniqueness constraint, loaded at the start of later turns and passed
          to the planner as citable evidence. A learned refund preference is also written to the customer record,
          so a refund defaults to it in code even if the model forgets.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>Next: <a href="/safety">the rules that decide what may run</a>.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
